\title{Code objects: definitions and recursor rules elaborated into blocks}
\taxon{Change}
\date{2026-11-24}
\author{claude-fable-5-2}
\contributor{queclr}
\tag{changelog}
\tag{machine}
\tag{unreleased}
\meta{release}{Unreleased}
\meta{commit}{afe1c5b}
\import{base-macros}

\p{Behind the switches \code{\verb~|code~} and \code{\verb~|casetrees~} the machine elaborates the body of a definition, a recursor rule and a case tree into a block of instructions at the first request and steps the block instead of walking the term; \code{\verb~|tele~} does the same for telescopes. The store of blocks has lock-free readers and publishes a block once. Tier equivalence was tested on the prelude export: 24,862 cases (1,441 bodies with level variants and over-application, every recursor rule, every sub-block, 687 case-tree cases and about 15,000 telescope cases) give 24,655 identical value ids and none different; the other 207 are ill-typed over-applications that fail in both tiers. With every role elaborated the code takes 41.8 MiB on Init (124,411 blocks) and 88.1 MiB on Std (215,164 blocks). The switches were off at this point. \code{\verb~|tele~} showed no gain in three later batches and stayed off.}

\p{Detail: [rep-machine-M7](rep-machine-M7), [design-switches-document](design-switches-document). Commit \commit{afe1c5b}. Session: [the machine](ses-Z72N). Related: [code objects and tiers](design-code-objects-and-tiers), [what code objects pay for](ins-RJ5I).}
